+# Why every field is a pill — and the one-line-per-theme fix
+
+## What `--radius-field` actually is
+
+In Scaffold-ETH 2 the UI layer is **DaisyUI 5** running on **Tailwind CSS 4**.
+DaisyUI 5 is token-driven: each theme declared in a `@plugin "daisyui/theme"`
+block sets a handful of CSS custom properties that DaisyUI's own component
+classes read at render time. The three radius tokens are:
+
+- `--radius-selector` → checkboxes, toggles, radios, badges
+- `--radius-field` → **the "field/button" family: `.input`, `.select`, `.textarea`, `.btn`, `.file-input`, `.join` items, `.tab`, …**
+- `--radius-box` → cards, modals, alerts
+
+DaisyUI's stylesheet defines those component classes roughly as:
+
+```css
+.input, .select, .textarea { border-radius: var(--radius-field); }
+```
+
+So the corner radius of every field in the app is **not** hardcoded in the
+component — it is *inherited from the theme token*. Whatever you put in
+`--radius-field` is the radius of every input, select, and textarea.
+
+## Why it reshapes *everything*
+
+`--radius-field: 9999rem;` is an effectively-infinite radius. For a
+single-line control (input, select) an oversized radius clamps to half the
+height → fully rounded ends → **pill shape**. For a multi-line `textarea` the
+same huge radius bows the corners so far inward that they intrude on the text
+box, so the content **clips at the corners**.
+
+Because Scaffold-ETH's own building blocks — `InputBase`, `AddressInput`,
+`EtherInput`, `IntegerInput`, the network/select widgets — all render the
+DaisyUI `.input`/`.select` classes, they *all* consume the token too. The bad
+value is a single source that fans out to every field in the app, including
+ones you don't author directly.
+
+## Why the per-component `rounded-*` patching keeps losing
+
+The teammate's `rounded-lg` fixes treat the symptom at the leaf, not the cause
+at the root, so it's an unwinnable game of whack-a-mole:
+
+1. **It's opt-in, per instance.** The pill shape is the *default* (it comes
+   from the theme token). Every new form field starts correct-by-default's
+   opposite — pill-shaped — until someone remembers to hand-patch it. New
+   fields regress automatically.
+2. **It can't reach everything.** Fields rendered inside Scaffold-ETH /
+   library components deep in the tree aren't places you can drop a class on
+   without wrapping or forking them.
+3. **It diverges from the theme.** A hardcoded `rounded-lg` is disconnected
+   from the design token. Change themes (light vs dark), restyle later, and the
+   patched fields stay frozen at `lg` while everything else follows the token —
+   they "drift out of style."
+4. **It's a cascade fight.** `.input { border-radius: var(--radius-field) }`
+   and `.rounded-lg { border-radius: var(--radius-lg) }` are both single-class
+   selectors (equal specificity); the patch only wins on layer/source order and
+   sometimes needs `!` to stick — fragile.
+
+Fix the token once and all four problems vanish: correct-by-default,
+reaches every field, follows the theme, no specificity war.
+
+## The correct fix
+
+Set `--radius-field` to a sane, non-infinite radius. Match the `rounded-lg`
+the teammate already reached for so the whole app lands on one consistent
+corner:
+
+```css
+--radius-field: 0.5rem;   /* was 9999rem */
+```
+
+(0.5rem = Tailwind's `rounded-lg`. Any small value works — `0.375rem` /
+`0.25rem` — pick the house style; the point is a real radius, not `9999rem`.)
+
+### Every place it must be applied
+
+The token is **per-theme**, and Scaffold-ETH 2 ships **two** theme blocks in
+`packages/nextjs/styles/globals.css`. The value must be set **identically in
+each** — fixing only `light` leaves `dark` pilled:
+
+```css
+@plugin "daisyui/theme" {
+  name: "light";
+  ...
+  --radius-field: 0.5rem;   /* was 9999rem */
+  ...
+}
+
+@plugin "daisyui/theme" {
+  name: "dark";
+  ...
+  --radius-field: 0.5rem;   /* was 9999rem */
+  ...
+}
+```
+
+If the project has added any further custom `@plugin "daisyui/theme"` blocks,
+set `--radius-field` in those too.
+
+### Then remove the patches
+
+Delete the two `rounded-lg` overrides on the previously-patched inputs. With
+the token corrected they inherit the right radius automatically, and removing
+them restores a single source of truth so nothing drifts again.

